WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:06.049 --> 00:00:10.530
Malcolm Grant: It gives me great pleasure
to welcome Carolyn Downs and David Pearson.

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:17.530
Would you like to come up and join us? First
of all, while you are settling down, on behalf

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:23.570
of the whole Board, to say how really pleased
we are to have this opportunity to talk with

00:00:23.570 --> 00:00:30.509
you about adult social care. Moira, by the
way, continually reminds us that we use the

00:00:30.509 --> 00:00:33.410
language of health and social care meaning
health.

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:35.950
David Pearson: Good.

00:00:35.950 --> 00:00:42.950
Malcolm Grant: Today, as our physical manifestation
that that should not be the case. Just you

00:00:44.469 --> 00:00:49.120
to introduce you to colleagues: Carolyn is
the Chief Executive of the Local Government

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:55.320
Association, which is a membership association
of local authorities across the country; David

00:00:55.320 --> 00:01:00.679
is the President of the Association of Directors
of Adult Social Services.

00:01:00.679 --> 00:01:04.269
Can I just say, first of all, we are really
pleased with the work that we have been able

00:01:04.269 --> 00:01:09.920
to do together? We have been working together
across a number of areas and including the

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:16.920
Better Care Fund. You know and we know from
the Five Year Forward View just how much further

00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:23.620
work there is to do. We have had your paper.
We have had a chance to read it. We would

00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:27.540
like an opportunity to discuss it. Before
we do, we would like to give you an opportunity

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:32.170
just to draw our attention to some of the
key points. Welcome and the floor is yours.

00:01:32.170 --> 00:01:37.810
David Pearson: Thank you very much. We are
delighted to be here too. Clearly the challenge

00:01:37.810 --> 00:01:42.420
of making sure that we integrate our services
where appropriate will require leadership

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:48.380
at all levels of our respective organisations.
I think it is good that it starts here in

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:49.830
that sense.

00:01:49.830 --> 00:01:54.590
You are absolutely right about the good work
that we have been doing. In fact I started

00:01:54.590 --> 00:01:59.510
a multi-disciplinary community mental health
team 30 years ago, in integrating mental health

00:01:59.510 --> 00:02:04.040
work. It has a long, rich history between
health and care. Clearly, we need to move

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:11.040
it to another level. I am just going to say
three things about the Care Act, the workforce

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:17.780
and overall where social care is. Then Carolyn
is going to quickly lead us into the discussion

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:20.480
and the future.

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:26.960
This is a timely visit to the Board because
next week we see the most major piece of legislation

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:33.960
for social care in 65 years, which we flagged
up in the report, but has some highly important

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:41.310
provisions which affect both health and care
around advice and information, prevention

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:46.290
and earlier intervention, national assessment
and eligibility criteria, personal budgets

00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:53.290
as a requirement, not just a policy direction,
safeguarding arrangements for adults and the

00:02:54.010 --> 00:02:54.920
mental capacity act.

00:02:54.920 --> 00:03:00.800
The work that we have done with Jane and Simon
on transforming care for people with learning

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:06.100
disabilities is centre stage in that work.
Deferred payment, social care in prisons and

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:11.630
profound work around carers' provision for
the first time as a statutory requirement.

00:03:11.630 --> 00:03:17.870
Since carers make £119 billion contribution
to the health and social care economy, £55

00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:24.040
billion to social care, when the net State
investment is £14 billion, it makes a huge

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:24.780
contribution.

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:31.780
In year two, of course, there will be provisions
for extending the role of the state in terms

00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:40.750
of raising the threshold for people who get
state support for their care from 23,250 to

00:03:41.470 --> 00:03:46.849
118,000 and putting a cap on their care costs.
Which brings me to another quick point which

00:03:46.849 --> 00:03:53.050
is of course social care is not free at the
point of delivery for everyone. Indeed, if

00:03:53.050 --> 00:04:00.050
you are in the North East only 22% of people
pay for all of their own care. Whereas if

00:04:01.190 --> 00:04:08.190
you are in the South corridor around Hampshire
and down to the coast, 55% of people pay for

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:12.500
their own care, which does impact the market.

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:18.299
On money and workforce we have talked about
the impact of the last four years. I think

00:04:18.299 --> 00:04:25.139
it is important to flag up that the pay, training
and skills of most of the 1.5 million workforce

00:04:25.139 --> 00:04:30.729
in social care needs some careful attention
in terms of career path, pay, training because

00:04:30.729 --> 00:04:37.729
we have a 25% turnover in care staff across
the sector, 32% turnover of nursing staff

00:04:38.740 --> 00:04:44.509
in nursing homes, and we know from research
done by the Resolution Foundation's published

00:04:44.509 --> 00:04:50.999
report three weeks ago that it would take
£1.4 billion to bring everybody up to the

00:04:50.999 --> 00:04:56.400
living wage - £0.5 billion in home care and
the rest in residential and nursing care.

00:04:56.400 --> 00:05:00.599
One of the things local authorities have done,
apart from the wide range of creative and

00:05:00.599 --> 00:05:07.009
innovative work, is held down fees for providers
such that the Health and Social Care Information

00:05:07.009 --> 00:05:12.259
Centre data shows that they have actually
gone down in most areas of provision over

00:05:12.259 --> 00:05:14.849
the last four years.

00:05:14.849 --> 00:05:21.740
Where is social care overall? It is a mixed
picture in the sense that, in terms of overall

00:05:21.740 --> 00:05:27.669
satisfaction rates, they have gone up from
90% to 91% in the last year. If you look at

00:05:27.669 --> 00:05:34.309
the ASCOF indicators around performance, many
of them have gone up. How does that work in

00:05:34.309 --> 00:05:36.400
an age of austerity?

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:41.779
I think it does not belie the fact that there
are some serious pressures in funding as needs

00:05:41.779 --> 00:05:48.099
are rising exponentially and resources are
reducing. However, I think our paper tries

00:05:48.099 --> 00:05:53.159
to demonstrate that actually social care has
got a significant contribution to make in

00:05:53.159 --> 00:05:58.080
an integrated economy, not just because we
are important to our partners, of which NHS

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:03.669
is the most significant, but because people
actually value the service that they receive.

00:06:03.669 --> 00:06:10.669
At worst, our joint provision is regarded
as fragmented, episodic and impersonal when

00:06:12.009 --> 00:06:18.319
we get it wrong. What social care does at
its best is it makes it continuous, joined

00:06:18.319 --> 00:06:20.919
up and personal. Over to Carolyn.

00:06:20.919 --> 00:06:27.919
Carolyn Downs: On that positive note I will
become incredibly gloomy, if you do not mind.

00:06:28.169 --> 00:06:33.779
I think we raised a point about going forward
and, as David said and you have said, I think

00:06:33.779 --> 00:06:39.520
we are working together incredibly constructively.
I really do. That is nationally and locally,

00:06:39.520 --> 00:06:44.629
largely, so I think that is really positive.
However, our ability to continue to do that

00:06:44.629 --> 00:06:51.629
is collectively going to be determined by
how our financial settlements are dealt with

00:06:52.020 --> 00:06:53.249
in the future.

00:06:53.249 --> 00:07:00.249
You will not think you are lucky, but relatively
we think you are very lucky to have a protected

00:07:00.629 --> 00:07:07.339
budget. Our budgets, as you know, have reduced
by 40% over the course of the last four years.

00:07:07.339 --> 00:07:14.159
The current projections if adult social care
does not become a protected budget is between

00:07:14.159 --> 00:07:19.080
£13 billion and £14 billion total across
the country. That would be potentially about

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:26.080
a £2.6 billion further reduction if the budget
is not protected. That does not include demography

00:07:29.710 --> 00:07:36.599
and the demands on the service, which is about
a further £4.3 billion. You are talking about

00:07:36.599 --> 00:07:43.599
a potential £7 billion gap that we would
have in relation to social care. That, as

00:07:44.580 --> 00:07:51.099
I say, means for us that we have absolutely
no choice whatsoever but to work with whoever

00:07:51.099 --> 00:07:58.099
will work with us at a local level to try
to shore up public services and continue to

00:07:58.619 --> 00:08:04.199
give a high level of service, which is increasing
in terms of its satisfaction levels, interestingly.

00:08:04.199 --> 00:08:07.789
It is worth just that context.

00:08:07.789 --> 00:08:12.679
How do we do that? The questions are in the
report that we thought were well-worth a conversation.

00:08:12.679 --> 00:08:16.960
I just think there are some things we need
to be really, really honest about in terms

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:23.719
of our relationships and how we take it forward.
In the first instance we really need to think

00:08:23.719 --> 00:08:29.369
about Health &amp; Wellbeing boards and their
roles, their capability and their ability

00:08:29.369 --> 00:08:36.149
to become very strong commissioners in the
new world. We are very aware of the fact that

00:08:36.149 --> 00:08:40.469
there does need some strengthening and there
needs, in our view, to be a much stronger

00:08:40.469 --> 00:08:45.970
link between the providers in the NHS and
Health &amp; Wellbeing boards. We would really

00:08:45.970 --> 00:08:50.910
encourage people to have providers fully involved
in Health &amp; Wellbeing boards. We think that

00:08:50.910 --> 00:08:52.550
would strengthen them considerably.

00:08:52.550 --> 00:08:57.750
Then the second issue is local leadership.
We all know that both in local government

00:08:57.750 --> 00:09:04.750
and indeed in the NHS: it is variable. We
do not necessarily come from a position where

00:09:05.350 --> 00:09:10.569
we will automatically trust each other because
we talk different languages and we have different

00:09:10.569 --> 00:09:17.569
drivers. I think that issue of local leadership
is incredibly important to us all, going forward.

00:09:20.529 --> 00:09:26.800
We have spent many, many years talking purely
about commissioning and now there is a lot

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:33.230
of concentration on provision. At some point
we really need to bring together both the

00:09:33.230 --> 00:09:40.230
provision and the commissioning in a model
that works. When we talk about single commissioner,

00:09:40.310 --> 00:09:44.839
which is what we are very, very committed
to, it does not mean there just is one person.

00:09:44.839 --> 00:09:50.529
It is one place where the commissioning is
being undertaken. When we talk about integrated

00:09:50.529 --> 00:09:56.990
provision that could be one provider or it
could be a myriad of different providers.

00:09:56.990 --> 00:10:00.339
Those different models will be adopted elsewhere.

00:10:00.339 --> 00:10:04.970
I was in Northumberland last week. I know
it is one of the vanguards, which is great.

00:10:04.970 --> 00:10:11.970
However, that integration between the council
and the acute trust in terms of community

00:10:12.980 --> 00:10:19.470
and primary care as well as acute care, I
personally think is really fabulous. I am

00:10:19.470 --> 00:10:22.829
not sure where the commissioning role fits
in with all of that. I just think we have

00:10:22.829 --> 00:10:27.839
got some work to do still to bring it all
together, if we are just going to 1) continue

00:10:27.839 --> 00:10:32.300
to improve services and 2) drive out the efficiencies
that we have all got to do.

00:10:32.300 --> 00:10:39.300
Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much, Carolyn
and David. I will open up, now for discussion.

00:10:41.870 --> 00:10:42.690
I would invite Simon to lead for us.

00:10:42.690 --> 00:10:45.110
Simon Stevens: Again, thanks very much to
Carolyn and David. I would entirely echo the

00:10:45.110 --> 00:10:51.079
comments that both of them made about the
strength of the partnership work that I think

00:10:51.079 --> 00:10:56.360
is occurring now nationally and in many parts
of the country. Although I think we also expose

00:10:56.360 --> 00:11:02.240
the fact that that is not universal and in
some places we can see that there is more

00:11:02.240 --> 00:11:03.300
to be done.

00:11:03.300 --> 00:11:10.300
The questions that Carolyn and David pose
on the front of their paper, on the summary

00:11:10.850 --> 00:11:17.850
of the paper, are some of the key ones. It
is worth just mentioning three aspects perhaps.

00:11:19.170 --> 00:11:25.980
The first, obviously, is the overall funding
question. I have said all along that whatever

00:11:25.980 --> 00:11:32.600
the clear potential benefits of pooling funding
and integrating provision, the fact is that,

00:11:32.600 --> 00:11:37.629
to coin a phrase, putting to leaky buckets
together will not make a watertight funding

00:11:37.629 --> 00:11:43.399
solution. When the much heralded £8 billion
figure that people have inferred from the

00:11:43.399 --> 00:11:49.870
Forward View is talked about, one of the important
provisos for that was that there was not a

00:11:49.870 --> 00:11:54.660
further substantial offset in the availability
of social care across the country and to the

00:11:54.660 --> 00:12:00.540
extent that is the case. That will, of course,
produce more demand in the National Health

00:12:00.540 --> 00:12:05.779
Service. We have seen some of that over the
course of this winter. We have a shared agenda

00:12:05.779 --> 00:12:11.139
for ensuring that health and social care is
contemplated in the round as we go into the

00:12:11.139 --> 00:12:14.829
next five years and the next parliament.

00:12:14.829 --> 00:12:21.829
Secondly, that said, we do still have significant
opportunities to help each other raise each

00:12:23.199 --> 00:12:29.139
other's game and improve aspects of the way
in which our services are working. We have

00:12:29.139 --> 00:12:35.199
seen that some of the extra emergency admissions
to hospital over the course of this winter

00:12:35.199 --> 00:12:41.879
have been people admitted from nursing homes,
care homes, who with more targeted support

00:12:41.879 --> 00:12:48.879
by the NHS in those care homes could probably
have avoided the disruption that goes with

00:12:48.899 --> 00:12:54.060
being admitted to hospital, which is why some
of the new vanguard programmes are precisely

00:12:54.060 --> 00:13:00.829
about improving the quality and the comprehensiveness
of the NHS offer to care homes and people

00:13:00.829 --> 00:13:02.980
living in those care homes.

00:13:02.980 --> 00:13:06.779
The flipside of that is that there probably
are a number of people who when discharged

00:13:06.779 --> 00:13:12.790
from hospital the spend a short period of
time in institutional care and because the

00:13:12.790 --> 00:13:17.410
active rehab and intermediate support is not
there, in fact then are going to spend the

00:13:17.410 --> 00:13:21.779
next several years in care homes at significant
cost to themselves, their families and indeed

00:13:21.779 --> 00:13:24.560
local authorities. Again, we could change
the dynamic there.

00:13:24.560 --> 00:13:29.720
This whole system, how all the pieces lock
together, there is complete agreement that

00:13:29.720 --> 00:13:35.449
that is going to be an important thing that
we have got to solve and can produce important

00:13:35.449 --> 00:13:40.149
personal and financial dividends across our
current spending base and trajectory.

00:13:40.149 --> 00:13:45.920
The third thing I would just perhaps underline
is your point about workforce. That shows

00:13:45.920 --> 00:13:52.319
up as an issue in social care in several ways.
It shows up as an issue in terms of the knock

00:13:52.319 --> 00:13:59.310
on impact for the affordability of care homes
from the spiralling costs of agency nurse

00:13:59.310 --> 00:14:04.350
staffing which we were talking about a bit
earlier this morning. Having a comprehensive

00:14:04.350 --> 00:14:10.089
approach to workforce across the NHS and the
care sector is going to be very important,

00:14:10.089 --> 00:14:15.699
not just for the ability to recruit and retain
but to manage the cost inflation that otherwise

00:14:15.699 --> 00:14:17.439
care homes are receiving.

00:14:17.439 --> 00:14:21.499
In terms of the availability of home care,
we can see that a lot of local authorities

00:14:21.499 --> 00:14:25.769
are getting a lot more sophisticated about
the way in which they are procuring, moving

00:14:25.769 --> 00:14:31.649
away from zero hours contracts, 15 minutes
a time, expecting people on minimum wage to

00:14:31.649 --> 00:14:36.810
have their own car and drive from home to
home. However, is there an opportunity for

00:14:36.810 --> 00:14:43.310
the NHS, in terms of the care assistants,
the career ladders that we create, to make

00:14:43.310 --> 00:14:50.310
it a more attractive thing to be entering
the home care part of the workforce in social

00:14:50.949 --> 00:14:51.290
care?

00:14:51.290 --> 00:14:58.290
All of these are good areas for very constructive
joint working over the next year and beyond

00:14:58.999 --> 00:15:04.079
and, as we do that, as you said, David, we
have got some big things that kick in from

00:15:04.079 --> 00:15:11.079
1st April which we also are jointly managing,
not just the Care Act, the Better Care Fund,

00:15:11.689 --> 00:15:15.040
the Integrated Personal Commissioning, bringing
together health and social care budgets for

00:15:15.040 --> 00:15:22.040
individuals and some really interesting local
and sizeable initiatives of the sort that

00:15:23.709 --> 00:15:26.459
we are seeing being proposed in Manchester.

00:15:26.459 --> 00:15:33.379
Malcolm Grant: Please, let me invite comments
from colleagues around the table. Victor,

00:15:33.379 --> 00:15:33.910
please.

00:15:33.910 --> 00:15:40.910
Victor Adebowale: Your paper is clearly very
welcome. In a nutshell we cannot cut adult

00:15:41.319 --> 00:15:48.319
social care anymore and actually it says that
any further cut, even a sustained budget,

00:15:50.309 --> 00:15:55.759
still presents major challenges to the NHS's
ability to make any kinds of efficiency savings

00:15:55.759 --> 00:16:02.269
or indeed deliver the Five Year Forward View.
Let us cut to the chase here. That is a clear

00:16:02.269 --> 00:16:08.910
and present danger and it needs to be flagged
up and stated without any excuses.

00:16:08.910 --> 00:16:15.910
Beyond that there are questions about how
we work together. I can understand the emphasis

00:16:19.290 --> 00:16:25.649
on the NHS and local government working together.
However, it strikes me that the people that

00:16:25.649 --> 00:16:32.420
are going to generate the greatest cost, whether
they be old, young, are those people actually

00:16:32.420 --> 00:16:39.420
that fall between the two, who often receive
really uncoordinated services from a whole

00:16:39.459 --> 00:16:44.199
range of providers including organisations
like my own. I declare an interest. I am the

00:16:44.199 --> 00:16:50.740
Chief Exec of Turning Point. We operate in
250 locations. We come up against virtually

00:16:50.740 --> 00:16:56.029
every local authority piece going. I am just
intrigued as to what your thoughts are about

00:16:56.029 --> 00:17:03.029
how we build that coalition nationally and
locally so that people get seamless care.

00:17:05.170 --> 00:17:12.170
Of course, when we talk about the elderly
and care homes, I am increasingly concerned.

00:17:15.270 --> 00:17:20.290
If you go to somewhere like Leeds or Bradford
or Manchester or Wakefield, the demographics

00:17:20.290 --> 00:17:27.290
of that elderly population is changing rapidly.
I just wonder whether we have taken into account

00:17:28.339 --> 00:17:32.730
those issues as well. There is a lot in this
paper that is worthy of further exploration.

00:17:32.730 --> 00:17:33.150
However, I just thought I would flag those
up.

00:17:33.150 --> 00:17:34.050
Malcolm Grant: Do you want to respond?

00:17:34.050 --> 00:17:39.930
Carolyn Downs: We have to do it collectively.
I will respond after.

00:17:39.930 --> 00:17:45.930
Malcolm Grant: Okay. Let us get some further
contributions. Moira?

00:17:45.930 --> 00:17:52.930
Moira Gibb: I was going to ask a question
of Carolyn and David, whether they felt that

00:17:53.580 --> 00:17:58.580
their question E - how can we work together
on the sustainability of the nursing home

00:17:58.580 --> 00:18:05.580
market across the country? - do the care home
vanguards feel like fully engaged with social

00:18:09.130 --> 00:18:16.130
care at the moment? This is a comment, rather
than a question. David referred to doing joined-up

00:18:26.560 --> 00:18:32.610
health and social care 30 years ago and I
think there are lots and lots of good examples

00:18:32.610 --> 00:18:37.270
locally where things have worked and people
putting enormous effort into collaboration

00:18:37.270 --> 00:18:44.270
and then the NHS moves on to something else.
They are abandoned often. Their work recognised

00:18:45.430 --> 00:18:52.100
and I am not sure that we have learnt from
that work that has gone on in previous incarnations.

00:18:52.100 --> 00:18:57.710
I think, linked to that, one of the things
that is not in their paper but that we should

00:18:57.710 --> 00:19:04.400
emphasise is that geography does matter. It
is great that we are being much more flexible

00:19:04.400 --> 00:19:10.320
around Five Year Forward View and responding
to geographies. However, local government

00:19:10.320 --> 00:19:17.320
is a more consistent geography than the NHS
has been and we should try and support the

00:19:21.280 --> 00:19:24.390
local authority as a geography.

00:19:24.390 --> 00:19:28.090
My other point is, Carolyn talked about Health
&amp; Wellbeing boards and I do think if you look

00:19:28.090 --> 00:19:35.010
at the Devo Manc we are very clearly recognising
that there is not just a Health &amp; Wellbeing

00:19:35.010 --> 00:19:41.300
board. There is a capacity. I do not know
whether Carolyn wants to say something about

00:19:41.300 --> 00:19:48.300
the ability again to create that capacity
locally to deliver from that joint working

00:19:51.520 --> 00:19:57.620
that Health &amp; Wellbeing boards gives us. It
is too often assumed that a meeting can deliver

00:19:57.620 --> 00:19:59.670
and meetings are just meetings.

00:19:59.670 --> 00:20:09.650
David Roberts: Carolyn, you said there is
often a lack of trust because there are different

00:20:09.860 --> 00:20:17.400
drivers, fascinating this subject of trust
in systems. And, to me, understanding how

00:20:17.400 --> 00:20:23.830
those drivers are that impactful when quite
frankly what we are trying to achieve is the

00:20:23.830 --> 00:20:30.830
outcomes, are not so different. How do you
therefore suppress or change the drivers such

00:20:32.920 --> 00:20:39.920
that you can impact the higher levels of trust
leading to better outcomes, which I think

00:20:41.920 --> 00:20:45.390
we all would want?

00:20:45.390 --> 00:20:52.390
Ciaran Devane: I think I understand in the
paper you are saying you have tried to retain

00:20:55.220 --> 00:20:59.990
a higher proportion of local authority spending
for adult social care but nevertheless it

00:20:59.990 --> 00:21:05.150
is a 30% cut, I think is what you are saying.
Then there is another level beyond that where

00:21:05.150 --> 00:21:12.150
it becomes unsustainable, however you judge
where it is at the moment, sustainable or

00:21:12.530 --> 00:21:18.150
not. Dealing with that has obviously required
a lot of innovation. Everything we are talking

00:21:18.150 --> 00:21:25.060
about is saying we need more innovation. We
need a different philosophy for the whole

00:21:25.060 --> 00:21:31.780
system as well. What are the lessons you have
learned? How did you do that because if you

00:21:31.780 --> 00:21:35.640
go back a few decades you would never say
that local authorities were brilliantly innovative

00:21:35.640 --> 00:21:42.300
and flexible and dynamic? Now, clearly, they
have to be. How did you do the shift? What

00:21:42.300 --> 00:21:49.130
are the lessons? What do you see works? What
does not work? What are those new philosophies

00:21:49.130 --> 00:21:49.740
which we are going to have to put in place?

00:21:49.740 --> 00:21:53.360
Malcolm Grant: You very kindly produced a
list of questions for us.

00:21:53.360 --> 00:21:58.970
David Pearson: Yes, we have returned them
with interest.

00:21:58.970 --> 00:22:05.970
Malcolm Grant: Perhaps you might like to turn
your attention to some of the issues that

00:22:07.810 --> 00:22:08.720
have been raised.

00:22:08.720 --> 00:22:15.290
Carolyn Downs: I will particularly start on
the issue of trust and drivers, actually.

00:22:15.290 --> 00:22:22.290
We should all be seeking outcomes. I do not
absolve local government from a responsibility

00:22:22.760 --> 00:22:29.450
in all of this but we do all come from a starting
point with specific vested interests. Some

00:22:29.450 --> 00:22:36.450
of the drivers that we all have relate to
our individual silos in which we work, payment

00:22:36.800 --> 00:22:43.800
mechanisms and indeed even that position in
terms of who is the authentic leader in a

00:22:46.070 --> 00:22:53.070
place. I think that is where some of the suspicions
come from. I should think the Integrated Personalised

00:22:58.620 --> 00:23:03.550
Commissioning programme is a mechanism to
truly change all of that because that actually

00:23:03.550 --> 00:23:10.550
puts the person in charge of their system,
rather than our systems. For me, that is probably

00:23:12.660 --> 00:23:18.040
going to be one of the most transformational
things that we would be able to do if, and

00:23:18.040 --> 00:23:23.800
it is a big if, we are able to be successful
in shifting the money and getting that money

00:23:23.800 --> 00:23:28.640
flow through from one part of the system into
a different part of the system.

00:23:28.640 --> 00:23:35.640
Also, if some of the vested interests, ourselves
included, are able to let go a little. I know

00:23:38.120 --> 00:23:44.740
that central government say they are not keen
on legislation but we get legislation every

00:23:44.740 --> 00:23:51.740
two minutes about minutiae, frankly. Ian said
to me once, 'Why is social care got so many

00:23:57.820 --> 00:24:04.270
more people using personal budgets?' It is
not because we are an altruistic bunch of

00:24:04.270 --> 00:24:10.190
people. We are now, of course. It is because
actually we were shoved into that place through

00:24:10.190 --> 00:24:17.190
legislation and regulation. Now it is just
normal. It is something that we all think

00:24:17.890 --> 00:24:23.270
we should be doing. I do think in terms of
behaviour change that legislation does have

00:24:23.270 --> 00:24:26.380
a place in that as well.

00:24:26.380 --> 00:24:31.630
Just coming onto the issue of geography, I
personally think geography matters a heck

00:24:31.630 --> 00:24:38.630
of a lot. I do not think a geography is always
static. The ability to create capacity, there

00:24:45.240 --> 00:24:51.500
are many places across the country which that
capacity is not there. The only way in which

00:24:51.500 --> 00:24:56.320
we can create that capacity is by actually
joining up from the local government perspective

00:24:56.320 --> 00:25:03.320
at a greater level. Now, my concern about
some of that is some parts of government like

00:25:03.740 --> 00:25:09.770
to see one model and impose it everywhere.
You cannot do that because different geographies

00:25:09.770 --> 00:25:15.950
and different demographies do require a different
solution. I am very clear that capacity needs

00:25:15.950 --> 00:25:22.640
scaling up. Even the scaling up, I do think
there is a big issue for us in terms of workforce

00:25:22.640 --> 00:25:26.890
and capacity anyway. If you look at the amount
of change that is going to have to be undertaken,

00:25:26.890 --> 00:25:33.640
for example, in Greater Manchester, the capacity
to do that from both at the NHS and indeed

00:25:33.640 --> 00:25:38.610
in local government - I think that needs some
serious consideration as to how that is going

00:25:38.610 --> 00:25:44.200
to be brought together. I think that is a
very fair point.

00:25:44.200 --> 00:25:48.680
In terms of the innovation in local government,
there is nothing quite like a burning platform

00:25:48.680 --> 00:25:55.680
to make you change behaviours. That is frankly
what we have had. There is no question that

00:25:58.170 --> 00:26:05.170
people have done and shifted and changed the
way that we operate, using digital technologies.

00:26:07.180 --> 00:26:14.180
Looking at some of the efficiencies that have
taken out of some social care provision, by

00:26:14.720 --> 00:26:21.600
looking at the mix of social worker to administrative
support, technical staff - all of those issues

00:26:21.600 --> 00:26:25.750
which are able to then actually drive money
out of the system.

00:26:25.750 --> 00:26:32.750
Once council, Kent, which is a large area,
just by doing that kind of better lean technology

00:26:33.620 --> 00:26:40.620
really, that has driven £33 million a year
out of their social care system. There are

00:26:44.420 --> 00:26:50.470
radical things that we can do. I think the
point that we would make is that you can only

00:26:50.470 --> 00:26:54.820
do all of that once. That is where we feel
that we are getting to is we have done a lot

00:26:54.820 --> 00:27:01.820
of that. Of course, you can continue to be
more efficient but you cannot take huge chunks

00:27:02.130 --> 00:27:07.360
of money out of the system more than that
once. I think that is where we are at the

00:27:07.360 --> 00:27:12.870
moment, very, very concerned that the next
lot of cuts will actually impact our ability.

00:27:12.870 --> 00:27:18.320
It is not just if social care is protected.
It is the other areas which lead to the preventive

00:27:18.320 --> 00:27:22.100
services across the piece.

00:27:22.100 --> 00:27:29.100
As you know, our public health budgets have,
for the last two years, been protected. We

00:27:29.390 --> 00:27:34.840
do not know whether that will happen, going
forward. If that does not happen going forward

00:27:34.840 --> 00:27:41.290
plus adult social care is not protected as
well, then that is going to be pretty devastating

00:27:41.290 --> 00:27:47.500
for both ourselves and indeed the consequent
impact on yourselves as well.

00:27:47.500 --> 00:27:50.540
I leave the other questions to David, if that
is alright?

00:27:50.540 --> 00:27:57.360
David Pearson: Thank you, Carolyn. If I take
Victor's comment about prevention and I think

00:27:57.360 --> 00:28:02.420
the Care Act does have a statutory responsibility
for local authorities for prevention and intervention.

00:28:02.420 --> 00:28:07.360
My view of that is that that has to be done
across public sector and other organisations,

00:28:07.360 --> 00:28:14.360
including the voluntary sector. Public health
prevention strategies are very important but

00:28:16.790 --> 00:28:23.150
it is beyond that. It is actually about overall
health and wellbeing and involves housing,

00:28:23.150 --> 00:28:26.030
involves community and leisure activities,
because our needs for health and wellbeing

00:28:26.030 --> 00:28:30.570
are not compartmentalised into health and
care alone. I think they need to be done at

00:28:30.570 --> 00:28:35.570
that level and Health &amp; Wellbeing boards are
very good places to oversee that because all

00:28:35.570 --> 00:28:38.920
those parties should be at the table.

00:28:38.920 --> 00:28:45.700
In terms of the demographics changing for
care homes, it is. The average age is going

00:28:45.700 --> 00:28:52.190
up. The levels of comorbidity are increasing.
We still have the design of care homes which

00:28:52.190 --> 00:28:57.980
is probably 20 years ago for a different cohort
of people. Often we think about nursing homes

00:28:57.980 --> 00:29:02.690
as being full of nurses. It is not actually.
They have got one or two for the whole of

00:29:02.690 --> 00:29:07.700
the population. We are talking about essentially
care staff and a couple of nurses. I think

00:29:07.700 --> 00:29:14.700
that emphasises Simon's point about workforce
and the work that we need to do to join up

00:29:14.780 --> 00:29:19.240
the strategies to make sure that there is
proper accredited training for social care

00:29:19.240 --> 00:29:24.040
staff and that there is genuinely a career
choice and opportunity across the sector.

00:29:24.040 --> 00:29:29.680
After all, the social care workforce is as
big, if not slighter bigger, than the NHS

00:29:29.680 --> 00:29:35.640
workforce and deals with a considerable amount
of acuity.

00:29:35.640 --> 00:29:41.220
In terms of the care home vanguards, it is
early days. We need to make it work. I think

00:29:41.220 --> 00:29:45.120
this is the sort of bit about leadership,
is it not? About making sure that whatever

00:29:45.120 --> 00:29:50.950
we jointly sign up to we make it work. It
will always be a challenge but that is our

00:29:50.950 --> 00:29:53.760
job and to do that locally.

00:29:53.760 --> 00:30:00.660
In relation to the comment about aligning
our performance and there was a comment about

00:30:00.660 --> 00:30:07.190
outcomes. I personally think that we ought
to move towards a shared outcomes framework

00:30:07.190 --> 00:30:12.330
across public health, social care and the
NHS. We have three different ones. It seems

00:30:12.330 --> 00:30:17.110
to me in the 21st century we should do it
jointly. I think there are a number of other

00:30:17.110 --> 00:30:22.980
incentives at a national level. We often talk
about what is the role of local? Actually,

00:30:22.980 --> 00:30:27.790
we need to define what the role of national
is as well. The role of national is to make

00:30:27.790 --> 00:30:32.610
sure there is sufficient clarity about the
policy, the performance and the outcomes,

00:30:32.610 --> 00:30:36.760
which actually facilitate local organisations
for getting it right.

00:30:36.760 --> 00:30:41.760
If we think about the Stephen Bubb report
on transforming care for people with learning

00:30:41.760 --> 00:30:46.830
disabilities, what he said was that it was
not because people were deliberately trying

00:30:46.830 --> 00:30:51.730
not to do something. They were keen to do
something. There were barriers in their way.

00:30:51.730 --> 00:30:54.930
Aligning those national incentives is absolutely
crucial.

00:30:54.930 --> 00:31:03.350
Finally, Carolyn has dealt with the point
on innovation very well. I suppose I would

00:31:03.460 --> 00:31:08.450
add that we have got a list of things that
people did in terms of creative work with

00:31:08.450 --> 00:31:15.450
carers, in terms of the technology, reducing
residential care. There has been a 13% reduction

00:31:15.650 --> 00:31:19.290
over ten years in the number of people in
residential care and nursing care as the numbers

00:31:19.290 --> 00:31:25.290
have gone up. We have worked very hard through
recovery and re-ablement to support more people

00:31:25.290 --> 00:31:30.430
at home. Creative use of personal budgets
- and I do reinforce Carolyn's point about

00:31:30.430 --> 00:31:35.290
the transformative nature of the personal
budgets - it is difficult to do. It is particularly

00:31:35.290 --> 00:31:39.310
difficult to do for the Health Service. However,
it is not impossible if we drive it through

00:31:39.310 --> 00:31:39.510
with determination.

00:31:39.510 --> 00:31:45.640
Of course, the other bit about the system
is to make sure that we have got excellent,

00:31:45.640 --> 00:31:52.000
fantastic advice and information at the beginning
so that people genuinely understand the conditions

00:31:52.000 --> 00:31:55.560
that they have got and circumstances that
they find themselves in and can help manage

00:31:55.560 --> 00:32:01.680
those with their informal carers. I think
our evidence in social care is if you get

00:32:01.680 --> 00:32:07.400
that bit right at the front end of the process
you can make a huge difference, not only to

00:32:07.400 --> 00:32:14.010
the number of people who subsequently come
forward but to the quality of their experience

00:32:14.010 --> 00:32:15.940
of services. Thank you.

00:32:15.940 --> 00:32:19.480
Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much, David
and Carolyn. I will not attempt to sum up

00:32:19.480 --> 00:32:23.190
all the richness of that discussion. However,
just on your point about personal budgets,

00:32:23.190 --> 00:32:29.630
we heard yesterday when we met NHS Citizens
one or two quite outstanding examples of what

00:32:29.630 --> 00:32:36.140
a transformative effect it had had in particular
on one young man. That I think behoves us

00:32:36.140 --> 00:32:42.460
to trust people to determine their own futures
and the way in which they invest money, rather

00:32:42.460 --> 00:32:46.540
than for us to prescribe what is best for
them.

00:32:46.540 --> 00:32:53.540
I think also we take on board your comments
about a single overall outcomes framework

00:32:54.100 --> 00:32:59.710
because actually we are completely interdependent
for a large proportion of the population in

00:32:59.710 --> 00:33:06.710
co-contributing to the outcomes that they
will experience at the hands of our two enterprises.

00:33:06.860 --> 00:33:13.860
Finally, I think to simply commit ourselves
to continued close working with you and I

00:33:13.870 --> 00:33:19.200
think every closer working because, frankly,
the vanguards do not work. If we take the

00:33:19.200 --> 00:33:25.320
care homes I think that is a model you have
spoken about where our interests are completely

00:33:25.320 --> 00:33:31.780
intertwined. However, also on the multi-specialty
community provision and I think on the vertically-integration

00:33:31.780 --> 00:33:37.980
provision. There is a real need to use our
joint efforts to try to shift the focus of

00:33:37.980 --> 00:33:44.980
care closer to people with full support, working
in the community and reducing the acute impact

00:33:46.620 --> 00:33:52.660
we are having on hospitals, largely or partially
through default of other models of providing

00:33:52.660 --> 00:33:53.350
care.

00:33:53.350 --> 00:33:59.260
I cannot say how grateful we all are to have
you with us this morning. It has been a terrific

00:33:59.260 --> 00:34:06.260
learning experience for us but also, I hope,
symbolic of our commitment to pursuing closer

00:34:06.360 --> 00:34:07.070
working.

00:34:07.070 --> 00:34:14.070
Moira Gibb: Can I just say one thing, Malcolm,
which is I think we should commit to doing

00:34:20.970 --> 00:34:23.389
it again? We should also recognise that children
are also not represented. I know Carolyn can

00:34:23.389 --> 00:34:25.159
obviously speak for local authorities but
we should think about that as well.

00:34:25.159 --> 00:34:28.520
Malcolm Grant: At this stage of the meeting
I want to invoke the first rule of chairmanship

00:34:28.520 --> 00:34:35.300
which is that after two hours everybody needs
a break. May I propose that we break for about

00:34:35.300 --> 00:34:38.790
ten minutes? Any dissent? No, the motion is
carried. Thank you.

